P and NP are two classes of problems that help use understand teh efficiency of algorithms:
P (Polynomial Time) Covers the list of problems who's solutions we can solve in linear time. Sorting a list for example, as the list grows in length, so does the time it takes to sort it.
NP (Non-Deterministic Polynomial Time) Class of problems fro which a solution can be checked quickly, even if finding the solution itself might be hard, or finding a fast solution might be impossible.

Jumbo John is a popular extended version of John the Ripper
***
Basic Syntax:
    john [options] [file path]
***
John has builtin features to identify the hash type. This can be unreliable, but if you can't identify what type of hash it is yourself, use:
john --wordlist=[path] [file]

If john can't discern what the kind of hash is, you can use hasher.com or hash-identifier. To use hash identifier:
use wget or curl to download hash-id.py from its Gitlab page, launch it with python3 hash-id.py, and enter the hash

--format=[type] tells john what type of hash you have
    Note that you'll need to preface it with 'raw-'
john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist......

This doesn't always apply though, so you can use this to figure out if you need to use raw:
john --list=formats | grep -iF "<thingtosearchfor>"

To show the cracked passwords:
john --show --format=<> <filename just cracked>

WINDOWS
Uses NThash to store user and service passwords. AKA NTLM

Cracking the /etc/shadow file:
You'll typically need root level access to see this file

John is particular, so you'll need /etc/passwd too

Once you have this, you can use a tool in John's suite of tools called 'unshadow'

Basic unshadow syntax:
  unshadow [path to passwd] [path to shadow] > unshadowed.txt

Word Mangling
John provides it
It's called single crack mode

GECOS is UNIX for something with the OS. 
John is compatible with UNIX:
  When looking at the entries for the users in both /etc/shadow and /etc/passwd ->
  The FIFTH/5TH field in each is the GECOS field
  It sroes general information about the user-like their full name, office number, and phone number. 
  John can take that information and add it to its word mangling wordlist

To use Single Crack Mode:
john --single --format=[...].....
(add the --single flag)
Another change:
You need ot change the format you're feeding john
It needs to know hwat to create a wordlist from
change the hash:
    1ef......
to:
    mike:1ef...

john can crack the password on password protected ZIP files

Similar to unshadow, zip2john converts the zip file into a hash format john can understand. Primary Usage:
zip2john [options] [zip file] > [output file]
then we can feed it directly into john

rar2john tool works almost exactly like zip2john but it's used to crack RAR archives
"RAR archives are compressed files created by the WinRAR archive manager

id_rsa are files that contain private keys used as passwords for SSH
john can crack these as well to enable the use of SSH to connect to remote systems

ssh2jon converts the id_rsa private key into a hask format john can work with. Observe:
It works a little different though. It's a Python program located at:
/opt/john/ssh2john.py
replace teh ssh2john command with python3 and run the file

or you can use ssh2jon syntax command on the CLI:
ssh2john <id_rsa privkey file> > <output file>
then feed the file you want to output to john